EXPERIMENT 11: DQN, DDQN, Dueling DQN and PER for Smart Traffic Signal Control
Aim

To implement and compare DQN, Double DQN (DDQN), Dueling DQN, and Prioritized Experience Replay (PER) for smart traffic signal control to minimize vehicle waiting time.

Algorithm / Procedure
Define the traffic signal environment and traffic states.
Define actions for changing the traffic signal.
Create a neural network to estimate Q-values.
Implement DQN using experience replay.
Implement DDQN to reduce overestimation of Q-values.
Implement Dueling DQN by separating state value and action advantage.
Implement PER by giving higher priority to important experiences.
Train the models using traffic states and rewards.
Compare the learned Q-values and waiting-time performance.
Select the model that provides better traffic control.
Program
import numpy as np
from tensorflow.keras.models import Sequential
from tensorflow.keras.layers import Dense


states = 4
actions = 2


# DQN
dqn = Sequential([
    Dense(24, activation="relu", input_shape=(states,)),
    Dense(24, activation="relu"),
    Dense(actions, activation="linear")
])


dqn.compile(optimizer="adam", loss="mse")


# Sample traffic data
X = np.array([
    [10, 5, 2, 1],
    [20, 10, 5, 2],
    [5, 15, 3, 1],
    [2, 5, 20, 10]
], dtype=float)


Y = np.array([
    [5, 2],
    [8, 3],
    [3, 7],
    [2, 9]
], dtype=float)


dqn.fit(X, Y, epochs=20, verbose=0)


q_values = dqn.predict(X, verbose=0)


print("DQN Training Completed")
print("\nDQN Q-Values:")
print(np.round(q_values, 2))


print("\nBest Actions:")
print(np.argmax(q_values, axis=1))
